== Few-Shot Segmentation

Few-shot segmentation aims to segment novel object classes given only a handful of annotated examples. The foundational paradigm traces to Prototypical Networks @snell2017prototypical, which classify query pixels by distance to class prototypes computed as the mean of support embeddings. PANet @wang2019panet extended this with prototype alignment regularization. PFENet @tian2022pfenet introduced training-agnostic spatial priors from high-level features. HSNet @min2021hsnet constructed dense 4D hypercorrelation tensors from multi-level feature similarities between support and query images. VAT @hong2022vat replaced the convolutional aggregation with a 4D Swin Transformer for longer-range dependencies. BAM @lang2022bam addressed base-class distractors by learning what not to segment. These methods share a common pattern: task-specific encoder-decoder architectures trained episodically, learning segmentation from scratch on limited supervision.

A recent line of work instead leverages SAM's pre-trained segmentation capability. VRP-SAM @sun2024vrpsam introduces a Visual Reference Prompt encoder that maps a support image-mask pair into SAM prompt embeddings. ProtoSAM @ayzenberg2025protosam combines DINOv2 features with prototypical matching to localize the target, then feeds the coarse result to SAM as spatial prompts. Both generate prompts in a single forward pass. Our approach departs from this pattern by learning an iterative prompt policy through reinforcement learning, where the agent observes SAM's intermediate mask output at each step and selects corrective prompts that progressively refine the segmentation, enabling recovery from poor initial prompts on complex object geometries within the FSS-1000 @li2020fss benchmark.
